# Cook recipe reference
# ---------------------
# This bundled recipe is both a working example and a syntax guide.
#
# Comments:
# - Standalone lines that begin with `#` are ignored by the parser.
# - Comment lines are allowed before the outline, inside the outline, and
#   between content blocks.
# - Raw file bodies are not parsed for comments, so Markdown headings like
#   `# Hello` stay intact inside generated files.
#
# Recipe layout:
# - A recipe starts with a structure outline.
# - Indentation defines parent and child entries.
# - Use spaces only. Tabs are invalid.
# - Leave one blank line after the outline before any file content blocks.
#
# File and directory inference:
# - Entries with children are always directories.
# - Leaf entries that contain a `.` are treated as files.
# - Dotfiles such as `.gitignore` are treated as files.
# - Extensionless leaf entries such as `Dockerfile` are treated as directories
#   unless a content block targets them, which forces them to become files.
#
# Variables:
# - Use `{{name}}` in path segments, content block headers, and file bodies.
# - Variable names use letters, numbers, `_`, and `-`, and must start with a
#   letter or underscore.
#
# Expansions:
# - `{{api,web,docs}}` expands a comma-separated list.
# - `{{1..3}}` expands an inclusive numeric range.
# - `{{0..10..2}}` expands a numeric range with an explicit step.
# - `{{a..c}}` expands an inclusive alphabetical range.
# - Expansions work in path segments and content block headers.
# - File bodies only interpolate named variables, so expansions inside bodies
#   remain literal text.
#
# Content blocks:
# - Each block starts with a file header line.
# - The next line must be exactly `---`.
# - Everything after that belongs to the file body until the next header.
# - If a filename appears more than once in the outline, use its full relative
#   path in the header to avoid ambiguity.
#
# Inline expressions:
# - The CLI also supports small inline recipes such as:
#   cook '{{project}} / src README.md'
# - Inline expressions only describe structure. They do not support file bodies.
#
# This example uses variables, list expansions, numeric ranges, alphabetical
# ranges, duplicate filenames, and an extensionless file.

{{project}}
  src
    components
      {{Button,Card}}.tsx
    index.ts
  docs
    README.md
    intro.md
    section-{{a..c}}.md
    api
      v{{1..3}}
        overview.md
  packages
    {{api,web,docs}}
      README.md
  scripts
    deploy-{{dev,prod}}.sh
  README.md
  package.json
  Dockerfile

{{project}}/README.md
---
# {{project}}

Created from Cook's bundled `reference` recipe.

This file demonstrates variables in file bodies.

index.ts
---
console.log('Hello from {{project}}');

intro.md
---
# Introduction

Welcome to {{project}}.

{{project}}/docs/README.md
---
# Docs index

This block uses a full relative path because `README.md` appears more than once.

package.json
---
{
  "name": "{{project}}",
  "private": true
}

Dockerfile
---
FROM node:22-alpine
